INT. ROOFTOP AVIARY, HOTEL - DAWN

Wire cages. Cooing. A dead parrot on the floor in a puddle of seed. Gray light.

DETECTIVE RAVI SANDHU, forties, rumpled trench, kneels by the body. LORNA FITCH, fifties, the hotel's head of security, stands with a clipboard. MR. ASHWORTH, sixties, the owner, wrings his hands in the corner. BETTINA, twenty, a chambermaid, clutches a mop.

SANDHU
Who found it?

BETTINA
I did, sir. At five.

SANDHU
Door locked?

LORNA
Always. Keys: me, Mr. Ashworth, the bird handler.

ASHWORTH
He's on holiday.

SANDHU
Convenient.

Sandhu turns the parrot over. A tiny tag around its leg reads Percival. He freezes.

SANDHU (CONT'D)
Everyone out.

They scatter, Bettina first, Ashworth tripping on his own cane, Lorna last, throwing a look back as the door swings shut.
